WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.780
KENNETH CALVERT: The history
of the Roman Republic

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.030
is a cautionary tale.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.766
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.510
We are going on a journey
that will teach us

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.613
a great many things
about a free society.

00:00:19.613 --> 00:00:21.030
CARL YOUNG: The
Roman Republic was

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.310
one of the finest
systems of government

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.280
that the world has ever known.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.160
The reason we should
study the ancients

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.128
is because it makes us
better human beings.

00:00:32.128 --> 00:00:34.170
KENNETH CALVERT: In the
progressive modern world,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.860
we want to think that human
beings can be perfected.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.590
But really, what
history teaches us

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.370
is that we're the same people.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.920
If we, as a free
society, think that we

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.460
are facing unusual
tensions and events,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.780
you've got to understand
that there were mobs in Rome.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.830
There was all kinds of
trouble that they were facing.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.050
We don't have to be Rome.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.000
We learned from
the Roman Republic

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.430
the lessons of citizenship,
the lessons of tyranny,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.910
and what it all means
for a free society

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.910
to face difficult
times, particularly

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:19.522
if those difficult times are
a result of their own success.

00:01:19.522 --> 00:01:21.480
CARL YOUNG: The histories
are themselves a kind

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.300
of moral education, a
political education.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.460
We, as a nation, would
be far better off

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.430
if we spent more of our
time studying those examples

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.340
from Roman history.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.170
LARRY P. ARNN: Why
should you take the time

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.100
to study these old and
complicated things?

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.560
The answer is it's the best
single thing you can do.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.970
KENNETH CALVERT: The study
of the Roman Republic

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.520
is really one of the
most important things

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.380
we can do as Americans.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.670
Our founders-- Washington,
Jefferson, Adams, Madison--

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.120
all studied the Roman
Republic as they

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.130
created the American republic.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.550
It's very important for
us to understand why.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.700
This is our course on
the Roman Republic.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.490
Let's begin.

